(FLW-37 (see Discrepancy 1): the manual's usage-guard example
 (LANGUAGE.md:2321-2323) verbatim. It compiles and, with no arguments,
 prints the usage line and exits 1 - which is why the example looks
 correct. What it does NOT do is guard: `Print "Usage..."`'s period closes
 the If, so `Exit 1.` is a top-level statement that runs whatever the
 condition was. D1.vox is the arg-free repro of that half.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox FLW-37.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   Usage: ./program <file>
   exit 1
)
If arguments's empty then,
    Print "Usage: ./program <file>".
    Exit 1.
